From 68a37954b0237b3fa4008d4b4bcb42094f079899 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tony Garcia <180770769+loafecho@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 04:25:02 +0000
Subject: [RFC PATCH 2/2] hid: ipts: bound the stop path and never unload with
 a live HID device

ipts_remove() runs on rmmod and also on every reboot, power-off and
suspend (mei_me_shutdown() and mei_me_pci_suspend() both call
mei_stop(), which removes every ME client device). Its stop path had no
bound: ipts_thread_stop() waited for the receiver thread with
wait_for_completion() and no timeout. If the receiver thread died in an
oops, or slept forever on message_lock held by a task that died, every
later stop hung: reboot (captured from pstore: device_shutdown >
pci_device_shutdown > mei_me_shutdown > mei_stop > ... > ipts_remove >
... > ipts_thread_stop > wait_for_completion), power-off and rmmod.
Suspend takes the same path through mei_me_pci_suspend() and is
expected to hang the same way; that was not observed.

In addition, when the receiver thread returned an error from its
shutdown handshake, _ipts_control_stop() and ipts_control_stop()
returned early and never called ipts_hid_free(). The module then
unloaded with its hid_device still registered; the next open or ioctl
of its hidraw node ran freed module code (BUG: unable to handle page
fault, RIP hidraw_open+0xd8 or inside __hid_hw_raw_request,
[last unloaded: ipts]).

Changes:
- Wait at most 20 s in ipts_thread_stop(). Report a thread that died
  (PF_EXITING) or is still stuck, and mark the context "wedged": free
  nothing it can still touch, keep the HID device, leak the context and
  the MEI device, and pin the module with try_module_get() (if the
  module is already going away, block remove rather than free code a
  thread still runs). Log one error line.
- Pin the task_struct across the stop, and never store a
  kthread_create() error as a thread.
- Never wait for message_lock forever: take it in trylock loops bounded
  at 1 s, and declare it lost after three timeouts with the same
  holder. Sleep in ipts_mei_recv() on a sequence counter instead of
  taking the lock inside the wait_event() condition. Send with
  mei_cldev_send_timeout(), and don't retry on -EINTR (the message is
  already queued).
- Count HID callbacks; when stopping, wait at most 10 s for them and
  release a pending GET_FEATURE at once. Treat a callback that never
  returns (its task died inside hidraw_ioctl() holding the HID core's
  minors_rwsem) as wedged, because hid_destroy_device() would block
  forever on that lock.
- Run ipts_hid_free() on every other path, including a failed stop
  handshake. If the device never confirmed the stop, or was never told
  to stop, leak its DMA buffers instead of freeing them.
- Unwind everything probe and start set up on error, stopping the
  receiver before freeing anything it uses.
- Run the EDS1 mode-switch restart from a work item serialized with
  remove (disable_work_sync()), never inside the HID callback.
- Check should_stop inside the receiver's inner loops, and never let
  the poll loop walk more than IPTS_BUFFERS behind the doorbell.
- On every stop, wait up to 3 s for the QUIESCE_IO confirmation, even
  if the last READY_FOR_DATA reply was lost; starting the device again
  before it stopped can leave its firmware unresponsive until the next
  boot.
- Remove the dead "timeout < 0" branch on a u64.

Tested on a Surface Pro 7 (EDS2, poll mode), kernel 6.19.8, with this
code plus a test-only fault-injection patch that is compiled out by
default: reboot with a crashed driver, driver unload during HID requests
(ipts-unload-race.sh), start-up race (ipts-race.sh), fault injection
(receiver thread or a caller dying while holding message_lock; a failed
stop handshake). Reboot with the driver crashed no longer hangs; 200/200
start-up race cycles, 0 oops; 200 unload cycles with a failed handshake,
0 oops, plus 2 firmware stalls (the device stops answering until the
next reboot); with the receiver thread killed or the lock held, sleep
still completes and reboot returns in 32-40 s.

Known issue, found in review and not fixed here: once message_lock is
declared lost, ipts_control_stop() returns -EDEADLK and ipts_remove()
frees the context. A lock holder that is stuck but still alive will
later call up_write() on that freed memory. Only the dead-holder case
was tested (fault injection above).

Fixes: 8e40dfbbf0da ("hid: Add support for Intel Precise Touch and Stylus")
Assisted-by: LLM
Patchset: ipts
